From 85ff00353d25d6eb7874d667fc579a4c69feeabc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 21:33:19 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] CEC: the keys of the TV's remote are taken as soon as the
 connection is open

Kodi took no key of the TV's remote, and no command of the TV, until the
TV said over CEC that it is on: the update thread asks the TV (or the
first device to wake) for its power status once a second
(CPeripheralCecAdapterUpdateThread::WaitReady()), and only then sets
m_bIsReady, which GetNextKey() and CecCommand() waited for. It is so
since 2012 (xbmc/xbmc 56ce04d94b, "some TVs don't like us querying it
while activating sources"), when the queries of the TV that follow (its
menu language, its name, the amplifier) were moved after that wait; the
keys waited with them. A TV that answers late left the remote dead for
that long: 92 s after a start, 130 s after a wake and 39 s in the
reports of issue #24; the keys pressed meanwhile came all at once.

A key comes only from a TV that is on and sends it to Kodi's input, so
it has nothing to wait for. Now (PeripheralCecKeys.h):
- the keys, and the commands of the TV that are keys of its remote
  (<Play> and <Deck Control>: its play, pause and stop keys), are taken
  as soon as the connection to the adapter is open;
- every other command of the TV is taken once it said it is on, as
  before: its <Standby> (a TV that is off, or switched off, while Kodi
  starts does not suspend or power off the PC through "When the TV is
  switched off"), its menu language, the audio state of an amplifier.
The wait itself, what it sends (active source, power on) and the
queries after it are unchanged.

kodi.log says when the keys are taken and when the TV said it is on.

Not tried on real CEC hardware. Tests: TestPeripheralCecKeys.
